  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how engineered nanoparticles can be directed to the brain at several targeting levels, from crossing the blood–brain barrier to accumulating in specific regions and finally binding particular brain cell types. It organizes strategies into four themes. At the vascular interface, ligands that interact with endothelial transport systems such as transferrin, LDL receptor family, insulin receptor, or GLUT1 are shown as ways to promote uptake and BBB penetration. Physical design is presented as another first-line control: particle size, charge, stiffness, and shape are depicted as tuning circulation behavior, vessel-wall adhesion, clearance, and margination.\n\nFor regional targeting inside the brain, the graphic highlights focused ultrasound and other external or environmental triggers that cause localized drug release or nanoparticle accumulation, including disease-associated conditions that appear linked to reactive oxygen species. At the most cell-specific level, different ligands are paired with microglia, neurons, or astrocytes. Examples include mannose or CD11b-related targeting for microglia, receptor- or peptide-based motifs for neurons, and several peptides or TLR4/LPS-related interactions for astrocytes. Overall, the image presents brain delivery as a layered design problem combining material properties, barrier transport, regional accumulation, and cell-selective recognition.",
